[Music]  this week on worlds stories drought hits  Kenya a country facing disaster hiv/aids  in Russia epidemic of denial but first  we visit Texas where there are barriers  along many parts of the US Mexican  border DW spoke to ranchers near  Brownsville about President Trump's  plans to build a wall the reaction was  mixed  [Music]  the border fence cuts through his land  no matter what rusty monsters ghosts  it's almost always inside Montez has  spent his life here on the border with  Mexico and all too often he watches  drugs and people were smuggled into the  u.s. monster says each year it has  gotten steadily worse that's why he  voted for Donald Trump and that's why  Monza's is for Trump's border wall until  Mexico cleans up its own government to  stop the and more of the illegal  activities we will need this wall  because the United States is a is a big  marketplace for illegal drugs for  illegal people two-thirds of his land  lies between the Rio Grande River which  forms a natural border to Mexico and the  border fence each day Montes travels  back and forth through one of the  transit points  Monza's suffers from cancer he says if  the government needs his lands to build  the new wall he's ready to sell actually  I love this property I was raised here  but I'm in a medical situation all the  stress and everything is not helping  what I've got just next door lifts  Pamela Taylor when the border fence was  built here ten years ago she found  herself living between the Rio Grande  and the fence effectively her home is  no-man's land  she knows every border guard here but in  spite of this she doesn't feel safe not  long ago drugs were found on her  property you have to be alert you have  to make sure that your doors are locked  you have to make sure that if you go out  your windows must be closed and  everything I mean there are so many  things that you have to do to make  yourself safe Pamela Taylor does not  want a border war between the u.s. and  Mexico  she says the old border fence has  achieved nothing Taylor wrote numerous  letters of complaint to lawmakers but  she no longer trusts politicians she has  a clear message for Donald Trump I  wanted to call him and I was going to  say you are doing exactly what our state  representatives did to us before their  bullying it in and we're going to build  a wall no no no you need to start  thinking about the people whose lives  you're going to disrupt before you start  building that wall  the 88 year old wants more patrols and  more border guards and if the government  needs her land for its new border wall  Pamela Taylor won't give up easily  because this she says it's her home  drought the animals are the first to die  then the children much of East Africa is  currently affected by arid conditions  unseen in decades Kenya has been hard  hit especially Turkana County in the  northwest Brown Creek water cattle  farmer caribou even yo walked half a day  to get here just to get something to  drink but there isn't enough water to go  around her grandchildren often go  thirsty food is also scarce many of the  family's cattle have already died people  here are surviving on a few wild beans  they've managed to find you don't want  much from any government we just need  food and water they should give us that  at least Caribous mother is weak she's  severely malnourished and in bad need of  medical attention but the medical  facilities in Tirana  are catastrophic most of the people in  the region belong to nomadic pastoral  tribes it's desperately underdeveloped  as a visit to a health clinic nearby  shows the door is open but there's no  doctor in sight antibiotics painkillers  and cough syrup lie on the floor and  open boxes it's disorganized and dirty a  man comes over to talk to us from a  school nearby  he says he's the mathematics teacher  there but he also works in the clinic he  tells us an aid organization constructed  the building and it's only recently been  open to the public he says he  understands a little bit about medicine  that makes him the doctor around here  you see even the register I do today and  up and up there is no there are he gave  me responsibility of looking here how to  hit calm yeah  it's unclear when exactly the doctor  will come by again Caribou opinion says  she doesn't rely on the Kenyan  government anyway the Turkana Nomad says  she just hopes it will rain soon  then the dried-out riverbeds behind her  hut will once again fill with water that  would save the last of her goats and her  family's livelihood 37 million people  worldwide are infected with HIV in  Russia the infection has reached the  middle class we speak with a woman who  talks openly about her illness  [Music]  three in the morning four at night  Auggie eremeyeva is 46 she's a  businesswoman and a grandmother she's  also HIV positive  Olga discovered her status by chance  after an accident put her husband in  hospital after surgery the doctor said  they couldn't figure out what was wrong  my husband had some virus at first I  thought it could be meningitis  it was HIV Olga and her husband had  lived with it for years undetected and  untreated within months her husband was  dead it couldn't be there was no way it  could be HIV that was always somewhere  else in someone else's life  I was always healthy always fit we cross  and Petersburg to the city's government  HIV center where Olga receives treatment  but how difficult is her case we read  you miss your witness engine we're now  seeing the epidemic entering the wider  population the kinds of patients were  seeing they're not drug users these are  people with families with children who  jacket or a medium you mean in most  cases they're being infected by sexual  transmission last years so reported HIV  infections in Russia topped 100,000 and  all-time high doctors estimate that the  true figure is significantly greater of  those patients that do know their status  only about 1/3 receive antiretroviral  drugs without them patients run the risk  of infecting their partners  Alden never considered keeping her HIV  status a secret it was never a question  for me I never thought about whether I  should be afraid of other people's  reactions when they heard about it for  me it's all about making sure the same  thing doesn't happen to other people  that's why she decided to tell her story  it's a timely message but one that the  official figures suggest isn't getting  through  our last report takes us to Berlin where  we meet Syrian Muhammed Ali Mohammed a  doctor of geography from Aleppo an  academic foundation in Germany has made  it possible for him to continue the  projects he was working on before being  forced to flee  [Music]  his world is geography  studying the changes in the Earth's  crust for two years the Syrian Mohamed  Ali Mohammed has been able to live and  work in Berlin financed by a fellowship  from the philips fonts initiative for  threatened researchers Muhammad taught  at the University of Aleppo until the  war in Syria put his life in jeopardy  once war broke out our apartment and all  of our possessions were destroyed and I  fled with my family and my parents to a  village on the border with Turkey from  there I regularly went to my work at the  University of Aleppo but there was  always the danger of being killed by an  air raid or by street fighting in the  winter of 2015 Mohammad was able to flee  across the border to Turkey from there  he contacted his former doctoral  supervisor in Berlin he had received his  PhD in Germany in 2010 but then he  returned to Syria they stayed in touch  I believe we have the advantage when  applying for the fellowship of having  long term personal academic and research  contacts dr. Adnan and the entire  faculty worked together on it the  philips fonts initiative supports some  70 scientists like mohammed throughout  germany it's founded in part by a  foreign ministry funding but also from  private donors the researchers come from  Syria Turkey Yemen and Sudan there's no  room for science mid war unrest and  persecution scientific work is  structured thinking and for that a  person needs faces of creative peace  which don't exist during times of war I  believe that it cannot exist in the face  of an immediate threat of war Megane  muhammad may be safe here in germany but  his family is not even though two of his  children were born here during his first  stay in Berlin my new  my family is now living in a refugee  camp directly on the syrian-turkish  border here in this corner that's the  border I hope that my family can come to  me here in Germany boostin but now  there's the problem that this border is  closed off by the military and at the  moment there is no solution yes  Mohamed would return to Syria but at the  moment he has no hope for a peaceful  future in his homeland all he wants is  to bring his family to safety here in  Germany  [Music]  you

